    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "We present our progress towards developing three-dimensional, discrete fracture fluid-flow models using geological observations and LiDAR data. To do so, we first determined the orientations and cross-cutting/abutting relationships of multiple fracture sets in an Austin Chalk road cut located in urban San Antonio. Next we conducted a LiDAR survey of this road cut in order to obtain a three-dimensional data set of the exposed fracture networks. We then mapped key fracture sets and used our outcrop observations to guide their extensions into the third dimension. When completed, this technique may, potentially, lead to improvements of existing discrete fracture models by more accurately depicting the spatial dimensions and connectivity of fracture networks.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Wilson, C.E., Aydin, A. and Sagy, A. (2007). Constructing Three Dimensional Fracture Networks From Outcrop and LiDAR Data. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: http://purl.stanford.edu/cw646fw5957",
            "type": "preferred citation",
